      "writtenOn": "2023-06-14T17:30:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I described this process to you incorrectly before. The CC terminations shouldn\u0027t change during a Hard Reset. I was thinking of something else.\n\nThe externally visible requirements on the sink during a Hard Reset are just that it doesn\u0027t draw too much current when the source reapplies vSafe5v. We have no way to measure that via an emulator.\n\nAfter PD starts again, shortly after the partner applies vSafe5V, the TCPM should initially be UFP and not VCONN Source, and you could test those things.\n\nThere are a few different ways I can think of to verify this, and I don\u0027t know which is best. Make sure the partner is Source/UFP/not VCONN Source before the Hard Reset with a Data Role Swap. The partner should be Source/DFP/VCONN Source after the Hard Reset. (You\u0027ll probably need to do this for each verification strategy.)\n1. Add an assert to the partner that checks the data role field on incoming messages and fails the test if it\u0027s wrong. (Probably in `tcpci_partner_transmit_op` or something in that call chain.)\n2. Run a host command to check the TCPM\u0027s self-reported roles. We do this a lot, but you would need to time it so that it happened before the TCPM did any swaps to change its roles.\n3. Alternatively, you could configure the partner to reject VCONN Swap and Data Role Swap after the Hard Reset. Then you could check the TCPM\u0027s self-reported roles at your leisure.",
      "parentUuid": "ca3fb10e_fc79d64a",
